<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.LibrarySystem
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:blockml="org.blockml.bml"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation">

  <idea>Libraries, packages, namespaces, versioning, and imports.</idea>

  <documentation>
    A Library groups related Block and ValueType definitions into a publishable unit. Concrete
    libraries use name Library, filename Library.bml, baseType org.blockml.bml.core.BlockLibrary.
    FQN = {package}.Library from the file's package path.

    Membership is defined exclusively by Library.bml _blocks — not by directory layout. A .bml
    file is a library member only if listed in that library's _blocks. Without a Library block,
    all .bml source files in the directory are public.

    Structure:
    • bootstrap — library entry point (Bootstrap block, typically README with readFirst)
    • _dependencies — other Library blocks (typical: inner library depends on outer)
    • _subLibraries — nested child Library blocks grouped under this library
    • _blocks — exported Block and ValueType definitions
    • _schemas — optional legacy XSD paths as FilePathEntity instances during migration
    • version on Library.bml — framework convention: version only on Library for org.blockml.bml.*

    Nested libraries may coexist at different package levels (org.blockml.bml.Library and
    org.blockml.bml.compiler.Library are separate units). Typical pattern: inner library lists
    outer in _dependencies; outer lists inner in _subLibraries. Violation: outer _blocks
    referencing a Block exported by an inner library → BML_LIBRARY_NESTING_VIOLATION.

    Namespaces: xmlns prefixes map to FQN package prefixes. BOM stores canonical FQNs only —
    prefixes are authoring convenience. Every BML definition must reside under
    &lt;domain&gt;.bml.* — no Block, Library, or other BlockML definition may exist outside
    the domain's bml namespace. See doc:BmlNamespace.

    Version resolution:
    effectiveBlockVersion = own version if present, else library.version if exported via _blocks,
    else error BML_MISSING_VERSION. bml:version on instance references is optional exact-version
    assert; forbidden inside Library bootstrap, _dependencies, _subLibraries, and _blocks.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see file="02_mbml.md">
      Library Format — structure, nested libraries, _blocks membership rules.
    </see>
    <see file="00_bom_core.md">
      FQN convention, domain-scoped namespace separation, instance identity.
    </see>
    <see file="13_bml_to_bom_mapping.md">
      Library discovery and TypeRegistry bootstrap from library manifests.
    </see>
    <see file="15_dev_setup.md">
      Monorepo package conventions and workspace layout.
    </see>
    <see block="blockml:Library">
      Published catalog of the BlockML core library.
    </see>
    <see block="core:BlockLibrary">
      Abstract base for publishable library descriptors — not exported via _blocks.
    </see>
    <see block="doc:BmlNamespace">
      Mandatory domain.bml namespace for all BML definitions.
    </see>
    <see block="doc:DocumentationModel">
      Next reading step — idea, documentation, references on Block definitions.
    </see>
  </references>

  <name>LibrarySystem</name>
  <type>
    <doc:LibrarySystem />
  </type>
  <baseType>
    <core:Documentation />
  </baseType>
</block>
